  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.707,
            0.05,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Geometric Twins"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.088,
            0.847,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Consider the downward extension of line l below QR. Would the arc from R meet this line downwards as well (as in the case of triangle construction when the sidelengths are given)? If so, would this lead to a triangle whose size and shape are different from ΔPQR, and yet has the given measurements?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.189,
            0.847,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It can be seen that the other triangle we get below is also congruent to ΔPQR. Why? Therefore, all triangles having these measurements will be congruent to each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.247,
            0.539,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, we conclude that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { A B C } \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm { X Y Z } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.266,
            0.846,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the case that we have considered, the parts that are equal to their corresponding parts in another triangle are"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.31,
            0.362,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) the right angle"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.334,
            0.846,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) two other sides, one of which is opposite to the right angle. This side is called the hypotenuse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.373,
            0.846,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "RHS (Right Hypotenuse Side)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " condition, and is one more condition for congruence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.412,
            0.718,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Conditions that are sufficient to guarantee congruence"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.431,
            0.846,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the discussions so far, we can see that two triangles are congruent if any of the following conditions are satisfied:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.473,
            0.679,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) SSS condition (b) SAS condition"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.499,
            0.357,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(c) ASA condition"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.498,
            0.681,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(d) AAS condition"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.524,
            0.358,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(e) RHS condition"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.568,
            0.812,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1.3 Angles of Isosceles and Equilateral Triangles"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.602,
            0.847,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Congruence is a very powerful tool for studying properties of geometric figures. Let us use it to discover an important property of isosceles triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.669,
            0.846,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ ΔABC is isosceles with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{AC},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { A } = 8 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . What can we say about ∠B and ∠C?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.721,
            0.634,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
